From 8c86b0cd37a577105aad3c73f29e6b1f31832969 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 23:32:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ask: XFRM/IPsec hardware offload

Adds the IPsec packet offload to the FMAN SEC (CAAM) engine: hook points
in xfrm_input.c, xfrm_output.c, xfrm_device.c and xfrm_state.c. New Kconfigs
INET_IPSEC_OFFLOAD (net/ipv4/Kconfig) and INET6_IPSEC_OFFLOAD
(net/ipv6/Kconfig), both depending on CPE_FAST_PATH from 010.

The IP output path carries its bypasses (net/ipv4/ip_output.c,
net/ipv6/{ip6_output,output_core}.c): a transport-mode frame for a
packet-offloaded SA (skb->ipsec_offload, the skb field from 010) is
still plaintext and leaves by the SA's own device. It skips LOCAL_OUT,
POST_ROUTING and fragmentation on the way there, since SEC builds the
ESP packet.

Also the CAAM PDB struct packing (drivers/crypto/caam/pdb.h) required
for SEC's view of the IPsec descriptor layout, and the xfrm_state handle
and its byh index (xfrm.h, netns/xfrm.h).

PF_KEY gets no ESN flag. It has no way to carry the replay_esn state an
ESN SA needs, so an imported flag left a valid state whose inbound
replay check dereferences a NULL replay_esn. ESN SAs come from netlink,
which validates and allocates that state.

The handle is the driver's: xdo_dev_state_add() sets it before the state
is inserted, and the state is indexed by it so that the driver can
resolve a frame SEC has decrypted back to its state. A state no driver
took has no handle and is not indexed.

GCM SAs offload like every other supported cipher; the shared
descriptor sharing policy that made them unsafe is fixed on the cdx
side (DNCPE-2358). GMAC does not offload: SEC's GMAC ICV leaves out the
IV that RFC 4543 authenticates, so the cdx adapter refuses it.

A packet-offloaded SA's frames leave only by the SA's own device, the
one that hands them to SEC: a bundle routed to another device is dropped
and counted as XfrmOutBundleCheckError, and validate_xmit_xfrm() drops a
frame moved off it later. A bundle of more than one transform is dropped
the same way, since the driver applies only the first. Tunnel mode goes
to the device directly with an Ethernet header the IPsec offline port
rewrites, so no neighbour of the inner family is resolved; the SA's own
next hop is still used per packet, as neighbour output used it, so Linux
keeps that entry resolved. A GSO packet is segmented in software first,
checksums finished, so SEC gets one packet per ESP. The frame's DPOVRD
follows the SA's mode: a tunnel's names the inner protocol, a transport
SA's the frame's own IP header length and next-header offset. A frame
the driver could not give SEC is counted as a transmit drop, not in
`tx toenc`. An update that would change a packet-offloaded state's
encapsulation ports or output mark in place is refused: no driver hears
of an update, and the hardware SA carries the ports and was addressed by
a route looked up with the mark, so deleting and adding the state is
what programs new ones.

A state with a handle is received through SEC and nothing else: SEC
keeps its anti-replay window, and xfrm's copy of it is refreshed from
SEC but never written back. xfrm_input() hands its ESP to the driver,
which takes only a frame that arrived on the state's own device, and
drops whatever SEC is not given -- another device's or another port's
ESP, a frame the driver could not queue, ESP delivered by GRO -- as
XfrmInStateMismatch rather than decrypt it against a second window. A
state another driver offloads is received as upstream receives it. Each
frame it does give SEC carries a DPOVRD describing its header as the
stack parsed it, IPv4 options and IPv6 extension headers included, since
the SA's PDB states a plain one: a tunnel's the outer header SEC strips,
a transport SA's its header up to ESP and the byte naming ESP.

A packet-offloaded state's sequence numbers and anti-replay window move in
its device, so xfrm's copy of them is the driver's to publish.
XFRM_MSG_GETAE and xfrm_state_migrate() ask it to
(xdo_dev_state_update_stats()) before they read that copy, as
XFRM_MSG_GETSA already does, and XFRM_MSG_NEWAE refuses to write it: no
driver hears of an update, so the state would report a replay state its
device never had.

Compiles warning-free: xfrm_state_lookup_byhandle() is declared in
include/net/xfrm.h and __xfrm_state_lookup_byhandle is static.

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
